    <item>
      <title>PK parameter model</title>
      <link>https://nmusers.github.io/docs/user-guide/predpp/population_model/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://nmusers.github.io/docs/user-guide/predpp/population_model/</guid>
      <description>&lt;link rel=&#34;stylesheet&#34; href=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/katex.min.css&#34; /&gt;&lt;script defer src=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/katex.min.js&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&lt;script defer src=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/auto-render.min.js&#34; onload=&#34;renderMathInElement(document.body, {&amp;#34;delimiters&amp;#34;:[{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;$$&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;$$&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\(&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\)&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:false},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\[&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\]&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\begin{equation}&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\end{equation}&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true}]});&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-container-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;headline-1&#34;&gt;&#xA;PK parameter model&#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-text-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-text-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Consider the following extension of the population model from&#xA;introduction. Each of 59 neonates was given some loading dose of the&#xA;drug, had a plasma drug level measured about 2 hours later, was given&#xA;maintenance doses about every 12 hours thereafter, had possibly one&#xA;trough level measured during maintenance dosing, and certainly one&#xA;level measured some time after the last maintenance dose. Due to the&#xA;long half-life of the drug, for the purposes of analysis all doses can&#xA;be regarded as intravenous bolus doses. The weight and APGAR score of&#xA;each individual are available as concomitant information. The APGAR&#xA;score (1-10) is a measure of a neonate’s health at birth.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>General linear model</title>
      <link>https://nmusers.github.io/docs/user-guide/predpp/general_linear_model/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://nmusers.github.io/docs/user-guide/predpp/general_linear_model/</guid>
      <description>&lt;link rel=&#34;stylesheet&#34; href=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/katex.min.css&#34; /&gt;&lt;script defer src=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/katex.min.js&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&lt;script defer src=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/auto-render.min.js&#34; onload=&#34;renderMathInElement(document.body, {&amp;#34;delimiters&amp;#34;:[{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;$$&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;$$&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\(&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\)&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:false},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\[&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\]&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\begin{equation}&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\end{equation}&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true}]});&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-container-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-3&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3 id=&#34;headline-1&#34;&gt;&#xA;General linear model&#xA;&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-text-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-text-3&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Recall that NONMEM supports general linear ODE model&#xA;$$&#xA;\frac{dA(t)}{dt}=K^TA(t).&#xA;$$&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;Here \(K\) is a \(n\times n\) matrix that could depend on \(x\).&#xA;\(K(i, j)\) quantifies the first-order distribution of drug from&#xA;compartment number &lt;em&gt;i&lt;/em&gt; to compartment number &lt;em&gt;j&lt;/em&gt;. NONMEM provides&#xA;ADVAN5 and ADVAN7 for this type of model. In particular, ADVAN7&#xA;targets problems in which \(K\) has real eigenvalues, suitable for&#xA;most PK applications.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Michaelis–Menten elimination</title>
      <link>https://nmusers.github.io/docs/user-guide/predpp/mm/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://nmusers.github.io/docs/user-guide/predpp/mm/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&lt;link rel=&#34;stylesheet&#34; href=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/katex.min.css&#34; /&gt;&lt;script defer src=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/katex.min.js&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&lt;script defer src=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/auto-render.min.js&#34; onload=&#34;renderMathInElement(document.body, {&amp;#34;delimiters&amp;#34;:[{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;$$&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;$$&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\(&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\)&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:false},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\[&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\]&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\begin{equation}&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\end{equation}&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true}]});&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-container-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;headline-1&#34;&gt;&#xA;ODE solution of Michaelis–Menten elimination model&#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-text-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-text-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;NONMEM AVDAN10 supports one-compartment model with Michaelis-Menten&#xA;(M-M) elimination. For M-M kinetics in other compartment models, user&#xA;will need to utilize general nonlinear models for numerical ODE&#xA;solution. In this example we use $ADVAN6 and $DES to describe a&#xA;two-compartment M-M model with first order absorption.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&#xA;Let &lt;em&gt;c&lt;/em&gt; be the drug amount, recall M-M model assumes elimination rate&#xA;\(c V_m/(K_m + c)\). Here \(V_m\) is the limiting rate at the&#xA;saturating concentration, and \(K_m\) the Michaelis constant. Using&#xA;$MODEL and $DES records we&#xA;can describe the ODE system as&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>PKPD model</title>
      <link>https://nmusers.github.io/docs/user-guide/predpp/pkpd/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://nmusers.github.io/docs/user-guide/predpp/pkpd/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&lt;link rel=&#34;stylesheet&#34; href=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/katex.min.css&#34; /&gt;&lt;script defer src=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/katex.min.js&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&lt;script defer src=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/auto-render.min.js&#34; onload=&#34;renderMathInElement(document.body, {&amp;#34;delimiters&amp;#34;:[{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;$$&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;$$&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\(&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\)&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:false},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\[&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\]&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\begin{equation}&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\end{equation}&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true}]});&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-container-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;headline-1&#34;&gt;&#xA;PKPD model&#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-text-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-text-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;PKPD model can be performed either sequentially or simultaneously. Let&#xA;us first consider sequential models in which PD is based on PK&#xA;results.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-container-headline-2&#34; class=&#34;outline-3&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3 id=&#34;headline-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;PK/PD sequentially modeled&#xA;&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-text-headline-2&#34; class=&#34;outline-text-3&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Assume a previous PK model run provides elimination rate constant and volume of&#xA;distribution prediction as data item &lt;code&gt;KK&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;V1&lt;/code&gt;, then we can use them in&#xA;the Emax PD model with an effect compartment. We see the&#xA;virtual effect compartment acts just like the peripheral compartment&#xA;in two-compartment model, so we can promptly use ADVAN3 to&#xA;describe this model. The data item DV from the data set is for the effect observations.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Plasma-urine examples</title>
      <link>https://nmusers.github.io/docs/user-guide/predpp/plasma-urine/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://nmusers.github.io/docs/user-guide/predpp/plasma-urine/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&#xA;&lt;link rel=&#34;stylesheet&#34; href=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/katex.min.css&#34; /&gt;&lt;script defer src=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/katex.min.js&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&lt;script defer src=&#34;https://nmusers.github.io/docs/katex/auto-render.min.js&#34; onload=&#34;renderMathInElement(document.body, {&amp;#34;delimiters&amp;#34;:[{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;$$&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;$$&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\(&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\)&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:false},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\[&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\]&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true},{&amp;#34;left&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\begin{equation}&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;right&amp;#34;:&amp;#34;\\end{equation}&amp;#34;,&amp;#34;display&amp;#34;:true}]});&#34;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-container-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;h2 id=&#34;headline-1&#34;&gt;&#xA;Simultaneous modeling plasma and urine data&#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;div id=&#34;outline-text-headline-1&#34; class=&#34;outline-text-2&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;We discuss several examples of simultaneous modelling of plasma  and&#xA;urine data.  The examples (examples/dextro) are adapted from a model for the analysis of&#xA;Dextromethorphan  data  provided  by  K. Mohammed Abduljalil.  The&#xA;first involves data from the parent drug.  The  second  involves  both&#xA;parent  and metabolite data.  In the control streams of these examples&#xA;plasma and urine data are simulated.  The same control  streams  could&#xA;also  be used for analysis of the data.  MU_ modelling is used for the&#xA;simulation to maintain consistency with subsequent analysis  runs  but&#xA;MU_  modelling  has  no  effect  on  simulation.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
